%3D%22465%22%20y%3D%22464%22%20font-family%3D%22Arial%2Csans-serif%22%20font-size%3D%2210%22%20font-weight%3D%22bold%22%20fill%3D%22%23b45309%22%3E%E2%9C%94%20Doubles%20flow%20capacity%20during%20storm%20%2F%20peak%20hours%3C%2Ftext%3E%0A%0A%20%20%3Crect%20x%3D%22830%22%20y%3D%22100%22%20width%3D%22335%22%20height%3D%22390%22%20rx%3D%2212%22%20fill%3D%22%23ffffff%22%20stroke%3D%22%2323495c%22%20stroke-width%3D%222%22%2F%3E%0A%20%20%3Crect%20x%3D%22845%22%20y%3D%22112%22%20width%3D%22305%22%20height%3D%2226%22%20rx%3D%224%22%20fill%3D%22%23e8f4f8%22%2F%3E%0A%20%20%3Ctext%20x%3D%22997%22%20y%3D%22130%22%20font-family%3D%22Arial%2Csans-serif%22%20font-size%3D%2212%22%20font-weight%3D%22bold%22%20fill%3D%22%2323495c%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3EINTERMEDIATE%20CONTROLS%20COMPARISON%3C%2Ftext%3E%0A%0A%20%20%3Crect%20x%3D%22845%22%20y%3D%22155%22%20width%3D%22305%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20rx%3D%228%22%20fill%3D%22%23f8fafc%22%20stroke%3D%22%23cbd5e1%22%20stroke-width%3D%221.5%22%2F%3E%0A%20%20%3Ctext%20x%3D%22855%22%20y%3D%22178%22%20font-family%3D%22Arial%2Csans-serif%22%20font-size%3D%2210%22%20font-weight%3D%22bold%22%20fill%3D%22%23142c3d%22%3EARCHITECTURE%20COMPARISON%3A%3C%2Ftext%3E%0A%20%20%3Ctext%20x%3D%22855%22%20y%3D%22200%22%20font-family%3D%22Arial%2Csans-serif%22%20font-size%3D%229%22%20fill%3D%22%23334155%22%3E1.%20Blind%20Toggle%20(Stage%201)%3A%3C%2Ftext%3E%0A%20%20%3Ctext%20x%3D%22865%22%20y%3D%22214%22%20font-family%3D%22Arial%2Csans-serif%22%20font-size%3D%229%22%20fill%3D%22%23b91c1c%22%3E%20%20%20-%20Equal%20wear%3A%20YES%20%7C%20Fault%20tolerant%3A%20NO%3C%2Ftext%3E%0A%0A%20%20%3Ctext%20x%3D%22855%22%20y%3D%22235%22%20font-family%3D%22Arial%2Csans-serif%22%20font-size%3D%229%22%20fill%3D%22%23334155%22%3E2.%20Fault-Aware%20(Stage%202)%3A%3C%2Ftext%3E%0A%20%20%3Ctext%20x%3D%22865%22%20y%3D%22249%22%20font-family%3D%22Arial%2Csans-serif%22%20font-size%3D%229%22%20fill%3D%22%23047857%22%3E%20%20%20-%20Equal%20wear%3A%20YES%20%7C%20Standby%20takeover%3A%20YES%3C%2Ftext%3E%0A%0A%20%20%3Ctext%20x%3D%22855%22%20y%3D%22270%22%20font-family%3D%22Arial%2Csans-serif%22%20font-size%3D%229%22%20fill%3D%22%23334155%22%3E3.%20Duplex%20Lead%2FLag%20(Stage%203)%3A%3C%2Ftext%3E%0A%20%20%3Ctext%20x%3D%22865%22%20y%3D%22284%22%20font-family%3D%22Arial%2Csans-serif%22%20font-size%3D%229%22%20fill%3D%22%23047857%22%3E%20%20%20-%20Wear%20balanced%20%7C%20Fault%20takeover%20%7C%20Peak%20assist%3A%20YES%3C%2Ftext%3E%0A%0A%20%20%3Crect%20x%3D%22845%22%20y%3D%22320%22%20width%3D%22305%22%20height%3D%22155%22%20rx%3D%228%22%20fill%3D%22%23ecfdf5%22%20stroke%3D%22%2310b981%22%20stroke-width%3D%221.5%22%2F%3E%0A%20%20%3Ctext%20x%3D%22997%22%20y%3D%22342%22%20font-family%3D%22Arial%2Csans-serif%22%20font-size%3D%2211%22%20font-weight%3D%22bold%22%20fill%3D%22%23065f46%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3EFIELD%20COMMISSIONING%20RULES%3C%2Ftext%3E%0A%20%20%3Ctext%20x%3D%22855%22%20y%3D%22365%22%20font-family%3D%22Arial%2Csans-serif%22%20font-size%3D%229%22%20fill%3D%22%23047857%22%3E%E2%80%A2%20Exercise%20unselected%20pump%20weekly%20to%20prevent%20seal%20sticking%3C%2Ftext%3E%0A%20%20%3Ctext%20x%3D%22855%22%20y%3D%22385%22%20font-family%3D%22Arial%2Csans-serif%22%20font-size%3D%229%22%20fill%3D%22%23047857%22%3E%E2%80%A2%20Stagger%20motor%20start%20times%20by%205-10s%20to%20avoid%20MCC%20inrush%3C%2Ftext%3E%0A%20%20%3Ctext%20x%3D%22855%22%20y%3D%22405%22%20font-family%3D%22Arial%2Csans-serif%22%20font-size%3D%229%22%20fill%3D%22%23047857%22%3E%E2%80%A2%20Latch%20thermal%20overload%20trips%20until%20manual%20push-reset%3C%2Ftext%3E%0A%20%20%3Ctext%20x%3D%22855%22%20y%3D%22425%22%20font-family%3D%22Arial%2Csans-serif%22%20font-size%3D%229%22%20fill%3D%22%23047857%22%3E%E2%80%A2%20Invert%20toggle%20on%20cycle%20completion%20(not%20cycle%20start)%3C%2Ftext%3E%0A%20%20%3Ctext%20x%3D%22855%22%20y%3D%22445%22%20font-family%3D%22Arial%2Csans-serif%22%20font-size%3D%229%22%20font-weight%3D%22bold%22%20fill%3D%22%23065f46%22%3E%E2%80%A2%20Always%20verify%20non-return%20valves%20(NRV)%20hold%20backpressure%3C%2Ftext%3E%0A%0A%20%20%3Cpath%20d%3D%22M408%20280%20h22%22%20fill%3D%22none%22%20stroke%3D%22%23227b92%22%20stroke-width%3D%222.5%22%20marker-end%3D%22url(%23arr)%22%2F%3E%0A%20%20%3Cpath%20d%3D%22M805%20280%20h25%22%20fill%3D%22none%22%20stroke%3D%22%23bd7b24%22%20stroke-width%3D%222.5%22%20marker-end%3D%22url(%23arr-amber)%22%2F%3E%0A%0A%20%20%3Cg%20font-family%3D%22Arial%2Csans-serif%22%20font-size%3D%2213%22%20fill%3D%22%23294758%22%3E%0A%20%20%20%20%3Ctext%20x%3D%2240%22%20y%3D%22520%22%3ESolid%20Cyan%3A%20Water%20Piping%20%26amp%3B%20Pump%20Flow%20(1200%20GPM)%20%7C%20Amber%20%2F%20Red%3A%2024V%20DC%20Control%20Interlocks%20%26amp%3B%20Contactor%20Commands%3C%2Ftext%3E%0A%20%20%20%20%3Ctext%20x%3D%2240%22%20y%3D%22542%22%3EDuty%20cycling%20equalizes%20operating%20hours%20across%20rotating%20equipment%2C%20preventing%20bearing%20brinelling%20and%20premature%20seal%20failure.%3C%2Ftext%3E%0A%20%20%3C%2Fg%3E%0A%3C%2Fsvg%3E",
      "alt": "Duplex Pump Duty Alternation and Lead-Lag Control P&ID",
      "caption": "FIG 1.1 // DUPLEX PUMP DUTY CYCLING & LEAD / LAG SEQUENCING: EQUAL WEAR BALANCING & STANDBY AUTO-TAKEOVER"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "content": "## 1. The Duplex Pump Dilemma: Unequal Wear vs. Fault Stalling\n\nIn commercial buildings, municipal booster stations, and industrial lift stations, pumps rarely operate alone. A standard design installs two identical pumps (**Pump A** and **Pump B**) piped in parallel across common suction and discharge manifolds.\n\n### Why Alternate Equipment?\n1. **Equalizing Wear:** If Pump A runs 100% of the time while Pump B sits idle as a \"cold spare,\" Pump A accumulates thousands of hours and burns out its mechanical seal within a year. Meanwhile, Pump B's idle seal elastomeric faces can dry out, and its bearing grease can oxidize or separate, causing it to seize the very first time an emergency occurs!\n2. **Thermal Dissipation:** Periodic resting gives motor windings, motor bearings, and contactor coils time to cool down below their maximum thermal rise limits.\n3. **Operational Verification:** Running both pumps on a daily or weekly rotation proves that both starters, pressure switches, and non-return valves (NRVs) remain fully functional before a critical surge hits.\n\nHowever, many beginner PLC programs implement alternation using a simple, blind flip-flop:\n```\nCycle Finished -> Toggle Bit -> Alternate Next Demand\n```\n\n**The Field Flaw:** What happens if Pump A trips on its thermal overload relay? The blind flip-flop doesn't care—on the very next cycle, it blindly routes the call to the faulted pump. Demand goes completely unserved, the wet well fills up, and an alarm sounds, even though a perfectly healthy Pump B was sitting right next to it doing nothing!"
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "variant": "warning",
      "title": "Intermediate Control Rule: Alternation Must Be Fault-Aware",
      "text": "A duty alternator should never be just a software toggle flip-flop. Real-world duty cycling must cross-check equipment health: if the duty pump is faulted, in maintenance lockout, or fails to prove flow, the standby unit must take over immediately without human intervention."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "content": "## 2. Stage 1: The Classic Flip-Flop Alternator\n\nIn Stage 1 below, we implement the foundational duty cycling circuit using a `PULSE_RELAY` (bistable toggle flip-flop) driven by a falling-edge detector (`F_TRIG`).\n\n- When water demand is active (`Call for Water` = ON), the currently selected pump runs.\n- When water demand is satisfied (`Call for Water` turns OFF), the falling edge triggers the `PULSE_RELAY`.\n- The toggle state switches, steering the *next* start command to the opposite pump via mutual AND gates."
    },
    {
      "type": "lab",
      "title": "Stage 1: The Basic Duty Alternator",
      "step": "Stage 1 of 3",
      "height": 520,
      "dsl": "circuit \"Stage 1 - Basic Duty Alternator\" version 1 {\n  call: INPUT(label=\"Call for Water\", value=true)\n  edge: F_TRIG(label=\"Cycle End (Falling Edge)\")\n  toggle: PULSE_RELAY(label=\"Duty Flip-Flop\")\n  not_toggle: NOT()\n  pumpA: AND(label=\"Pump A Gate\")\n  pumpB: AND(label=\"Pump B Gate\")\n  outA: OUTPUT(label=\"Pump A Contactor\")\n  outB: OUTPUT(label=\"Pump B Contactor\")\n  duty_b: OUTPUT(label=\"Next Duty: Pump B when ON\")\n\n  call.Q -> edge.A\n  edge.Q -> toggle.A\n  toggle.Q -> not_toggle.A\n  toggle.Q -> duty_b.A\n\n  call.Q -> pumpA.A\n  not_toggle.Q -> pumpA.B\n  pumpA.Q -> outA.A\n\n  call.Q -> pumpB.A\n  toggle.Q -> pumpB.B\n  pumpB.Q -> outB.A\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "variant": "info",
      "title": "Interactive Exercise — Stage 1 (Observe the Alternation)",
      "text": "In Stage 1 above:\n1. Notice that Pump A is initially running because \"Call for Water\" is ON.\n2. Toggle \"Call for Water\" to OFF (cycle complete). Watch the \"Duty Flip-Flop\" switch state and light up \"Next Duty: Pump B when ON\".\n3. Turn \"Call for Water\" back ON: now Pump B runs!\n4. Notice the limitation: there is no input for motor health. If a pump burns out, the circuit still tries to run it every other cycle!"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "content": "## 3. Stage 2: Fault-Aware Alternator with Automatic Standby Takeover\n\nTo solve the blind toggle flaw, Stage 2 introduces motor health interlocks from thermal overload auxiliary contacts (`healthy_a` and `healthy_b`).\n\n### How Automatic Takeover Works:\n- **Normal Cycle:** If Pump A is selected and healthy, Pump A runs.\n- **Duty Pump Faulted:** If Pump A is selected but trips its overload (`healthy_a` drops to false), the `takeover_b` gate activates instantly. Demand is immediately redirected to **Pump B**!\n- **Standby Backup:** The station continues delivering water without human intervention. An alarm indicator alerts technicians to service Pump A while Pump B carries the load."
    },
    {
      "type": "lab",
      "title": "Stage 2: Fault-Aware Standby Auto-Takeover",
      "step": "Stage 2 of 3",
      "height": 520,
      "dsl": "circuit \"Stage 2 - Fault-Aware Alternator with Auto-Takeover\" version 1 {\n  call: INPUT(label=\"Call for Water\", value=true)\n  healthy_a: INPUT(label=\"Pump A Overload Healthy\", value=true)\n  healthy_b: INPUT(label=\"Pump B Overload Healthy\", value=true)\n\n  edge: F_TRIG(label=\"Cycle End Detector\")\n  duty: PULSE_RELAY(label=\"Duty Selector\")\n  duty_a: NOT()\n\n  fault_a: NOT()\n  fault_b: NOT()\n\n  duty_a_pref: AND(label=\"Duty A Desired\")\n  duty_b_pref: AND(label=\"Duty B Desired\")\n\n  takeover_b: AND(label=\"B Takes Over for Faulted A\")\n  takeover_a: AND(label=\"A Takes Over for Faulted B\")\n\n  demand_a: OR()\n  demand_b: OR()\n  takeover_or: OR(label=\"Any Takeover Active\")\n\n  final_a: AND(label=\"Final Pump A Command\")\n  final_b: AND(label=\"Final Pump B Command\")\n\n  outA: OUTPUT(label=\"Pump A Motor Command\")\n  outB: OUTPUT(label=\"Pump B Motor Command\")\n  alarmA: OUTPUT(label=\"Pump A Tripped Alarm\")\n  alarmB: OUTPUT(label=\"Pump B Tripped Alarm\")\n  autoTakeover: OUTPUT(label=\"Standby Pump in Auto-Takeover\")\n\n  call.Q -> edge.A\n  edge.Q -> duty.A\n  duty.Q -> duty_a.A\n\n  healthy_a.Q -> fault_a.A\n  healthy_b.Q -> fault_b.A\n  fault_a.Q -> alarmA.A\n  fault_b.Q -> alarmB.A\n\n  call.Q -> duty_a_pref.A\n  duty_a.Q -> duty_a_pref.B\n\n  call.Q -> duty_b_pref.A\n  duty.Q -> duty_b_pref.B\n\n  duty_a_pref.Q -> takeover_b.A\n  fault_a.Q -> takeover_b.B\n\n  duty_b_pref.Q -> takeover_a.A\n  fault_b.Q -> takeover_a.B\n\n  duty_a_pref.Q -> demand_a.A\n  takeover_a.Q -> demand_a.B\n\n  duty_b_pref.Q -> demand_b.A\n  takeover_b.Q -> demand_b.B\n\n  demand_a.Q -> final_a.A\n  healthy_a.Q -> final_a.B\n  final_a.Q -> outA.A\n\n  demand_b.Q -> final_b.A\n  healthy_b.Q -> final_b.B\n  final_b.Q -> outB.A\n\n  takeover_a.Q -> takeover_or.A\n  takeover_b.Q -> takeover_or.B\n  takeover_or.Q -> autoTakeover.A\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "variant": "success",
      "title": "Interactive Exercise — Stage 2 (Simulate a Tripped Motor Overload)",
      "text": "In Stage 2 above:\n1. With \"Call for Water\" active, Pump A is running normally as the duty pump.\n2. Now toggle \"Pump A Overload Healthy\" to OFF (simulating a tripped starter thermal overload).\n3. Watch what happens in real time: Pump A shuts down, \"Pump A Tripped Alarm\" illuminates, and Pump B immediately energizes under \"Standby Pump in Auto-Takeover\"!\n4. The process never starves for water even during an active equipment breakdown."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "content": "## 4. Stage 3: Duplex Lead / Lag Sequencing for Peak Inflow\n\nIn real stormwater stations, municipal lift stations, and commercial booster systems, water demand fluctuates throughout the day. A single pump may handle 90% of operating hours, but during a heavy storm or peak morning demand, one pump cannot keep up.\n\nThis is where **Lead / Lag Sequencing** is used:\n- **Lead Demand (`lead_call`):** The primary demand switch (e.g. wet well reaches 50% level or water pressure drops below 45 PSI). The assigned **Lead Pump** starts and runs solo.\n- **Lag Demand (`lag_call`):** A secondary high-demand switch (e.g. wet well surges to 80% level or pressure drops below 35 PSI). The **Lag Pump** starts in parallel to assist, doubling discharge flow rate!\n- When the surge subsides and `lag_call` turns off, the Lag pump shuts down, leaving the Lead pump to finish the cycle.\n- On cycle completion, the Lead duty alternates, ensuring both pumps share the Lead role equally."
    },
    {
      "type": "lab",
      "title": "Stage 3: Duplex Lead / Lag Sequencing & Peak Assist",
      "step": "Stage 3 of 3",
      "height": 600,
